\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{tab:feasible}{{1}{4}{Shared-first feasibility, reported over every target. Full headroom makes all targets feasible; smaller ceilings reveal substantial rule dependence}{table.caption.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:success}{{1}{5}{All-target shared-first success versus edited-task budget. Infeasible targets remain in the denominator. Thus each curve distinguishes support efficiency from the maximum attainable fraction. A perfect-score ceiling is a structural stress test, not a demonstrated training capability}{figure.caption.2}{}}
\newlabel{tab:fixed}{{2}{6}{Fixed three-rule feasible nonwinner cohorts selected at $\rho =0.5$. Costs are mean optimal task counts, not median statistics. The last column is the paired mean difference $(K_{\rm med}-K_\mu )/n$ and descriptive target-bootstrap interval, with competitors and target IDs held fixed}{table.caption.3}{}}
\newlabel{fig:fixed}{{2}{6}{Ceiling effects on fixed cohorts from Table~\ref {tab:fixed}. Connecting segments only guide the eye. The HELM median's plateau is predicted by Proposition~\ref {prop:plateau}; increasing capacity reduces mean's required coordinate count. Open's even median can also change its support because the middle pair is coupled}{figure.caption.4}{}}
\newlabel{fig:magnitude}{{3}{7}{Different investment metrics on the same 77 HELM targets, shared first, $\rho =1$. Each point is one target; diagonals mark equality. Left: minimum task-count fractions. Right: independently minimized magnitude, not the amount spent by a ceiling witness. Median costs more tasks for 64 targets but less total gain for every target shown}{figure.caption.5}{}}
